    <main>
      <section class="hero">
        <div class="hero-inner">
          <p class="eyebrow">ADVOCACY</p>

          <h1>
            Your appointment.<br />
            <span>Your body. Your questions.</span>
          </h1>

          <p class="hero-copy">
            Practical ways to feel more informed, more prepared and more
            involved in your own healthcare.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="article">
        <div class="article-inner">

          <div class="section-block intro">
            <p>
              Being a patient can be intimidating. Sitting in front of someone
              who has years, if not decades, of medical experience, using words
              and terminology you might not understand, while you're expected
              to make decisions about your body. All whilst possibly in pain,
              anxious, overwhelmed or simply trying to understand what you've
              been told.
            </p>

            <p class="personality-line">Trust me, I've been there.</p>

            <p>
              Which is why it's incredibly important, especially as a woman, to
              know and understand your body so that you can be involved in
              making the right decisions for your health.
            </p>

            <div class="purple-callout">
              <p>Advocating for yourself does not mean you know more than your doctor.</p>
            </div>

            <p>
              It simply means being involved in your own care and listening to
              your needs. More importantly, you know <strong>your</strong> body
              better than anyone else. You know your norms, and you know when
              something isn't right.
            </p>

            <p>
              This means asking questions, understanding your diagnosis,
              understanding why a certain treatment is being recommended and,
              when necessary, asking for a second opinion.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="section-block">
            <p class="section-number">01</p>
            <h2>Before your appointment</h2>

            <p>
              It can be especially difficult to remember everything during an
              appointment, particularly one you may have been waiting months for.
            </p>

            <p>What has helped me tackle this is writing things down beforehand:</p>

            <div class="checklist">
              <div class="check-item">
                <span>01</span>
                <p>
                  <strong>Your symptoms:</strong> what they are, how and when
                  they occur, even if you don't think they're related.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div class="check-item">
                <span>02</span>
                <p>
                  <strong>When they started:</strong> whether they've progressed
                  or changed and whether anything seems to affect them, such as
                  diet or exercise.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div class="check-item">
                <span>03</span>
                <p><strong>How they affect your day-to-day life!!!</strong></p>
              </div>

              <div class="check-item">
                <span>04</span>
                <p>
                  <strong>What helps and what makes them worse:</strong>
                  including medications you've tried and any side effects.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div class="check-item">
                <span>05</span>
                <p><strong>The questions you actually want answered.</strong></p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <p class="small-personality">
              You don't need a colour-coded 47-page symptom spreadsheet.
              Unless that's your thing lol.
            </p>

            <div class="yellow-callout">
              <p class="callout-small">A QUESTION I ALWAYS ASKED</p>
              <h3>
                “How do I know when this symptom shouldn't be dismissed? How do
                I know when it could be something else that's serious?”
              </h3>
              <p>
                Chronic pain girlies will understand. When pain becomes part of
                your daily life, it can be difficult to know when a symptom is
                your “normal” and when it needs further attention.
              </p>
            </div>

            <h3 class="mini-heading">And absolutely do not minimise what you're experiencing.</h3>

            <p>
              If your symptoms mean you regularly miss work or university,
              can't exercise, struggle to have sex, can't sleep or have to plan
              your life around access to a bathroom, <strong>say that</strong>.
            </p>

            <p class="comparison">
              “I have stomach pain sometimes” doesn't communicate the same thing
              as “twice a month the pain is bad enough that I can't leave my house.”
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="section-block">
            <p class="section-number">02</p>
            <h2>ASK QUESTIONS</h2>

            <p>You are allowed to ask questions about your own healthcare.</p>

            <div class="question-grid">
              <div>Why do you think this is the most likely diagnosis?</div>
              <div>What else could be causing these symptoms?</div>
              <div>What are my treatment options?</div>
              <div>What are the benefits, risks and side effects?</div>
              <div>Are there alternatives?</div>
              <div>What happens if this treatment doesn't work?</div>
              <div>What does this scan or test actually rule out?</div>
              <div>What should I do if my symptoms get worse?</div>
            </div>

            <p class="personality-line">
              None of these questions are confrontational. They're normal
              questions about your own health.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="section-block">
            <p class="section-number">03</p>
            <h2>“My test was normal. So I'm fine... right?”</h2>

            <p><strong>Not necessarily.</strong></p>

            <p>
              This is where understanding a little bit of the science can
              genuinely help you advocate for yourself.
            </p>

            <p>
              Different medical tests have different limitations. A normal
              result can be reassuring, but it doesn't automatically mean that
              every possible condition has been excluded.
            </p>

            <p>
              Endometriosis is a good example. Current ESHRE guidance states
              that negative ultrasound or MRI imaging
              <strong>does not exclude endometriosis</strong>, particularly
              superficial disease
              <a
                class="citation"
                href="https://www.eshre.eu/Guidelines-and-Legal/Guidelines/Endometriosis-guideline"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
              >
                (ESHRE, 2022)
              </a>.
            </p>

            <div class="purple-question">
              <p>“What does this result rule out, and what doesn't it rule out?”</p>
            </div>

            <p>That's a very different question.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="section-block">
            <p class="section-number">04</p>
            <h2>If you don't understand something, say so</h2>

            <p>
              Medical jargon can make you feel like you're the one who doesn't
              understand enough.
            </p>

            <p>
              But you shouldn't need a biomedical degree to understand what's
              happening to your own body.
            </p>

            <div class="quote-stack">
              <div>“What does that mean in normal language?”</div>
              <div>“Can you explain why you're recommending that?”</div>
              <div>“Could you write that down for me?”</div>
            </div>

            <p>
              You aren't wasting anyone's time by wanting to understand a
              decision about your health.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="section-block">
            <p class="section-number">05</p>
            <h2>Bring someone with you</h2>

            <p>
              If you're nervous about an appointment, consider bringing someone
              you trust if you're allowed to.
            </p>

            <p>
              They can listen, take notes, remind you of questions you wanted
              to ask and sometimes remember information that you completely
              forget the second you walk out of the room.
            </p>

            <p class="personality-line">
              You don't have to do everything alone just to prove you can
              advocate for yourself.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="section-block">
            <p class="section-number">06</p>
            <h2>And if you feel dismissed?</h2>

            <p>
              Sometimes a healthcare professional may disagree with what you
              think is causing your symptoms, and that doesn't necessarily mean
              you're being dismissed. <strong>But disagreement and dismissal
              are not the same thing.</strong>
            </p>

            <p>
              You deserve to have your symptoms taken seriously, to have
              decisions explained to you and to be treated like a person
              throughout your care.
            </p>

            <p>
              If you're told that a particular condition isn't the explanation,
              it's okay to bring the conversation back to the problem that
              still exists:
            </p>

            <div class="quote-stack">
              <div>
                “I understand that you don't think this is endometriosis. What
                do you think could be causing these symptoms instead?”
              </div>

              <div>
                “These symptoms are still significantly affecting my everyday
                life. What is the next step?”
              </div>
            </div>

            <p>
              And if you repeatedly feel unheard or don't feel comfortable with
              the care you're receiving, <strong>it's okay to ask about getting
              another opinion.</strong>
            </p>

            <p>
              Advocating for yourself doesn't mean that a doctor has to agree
              with you. But you should be able to ask questions, understand the
              reasoning behind your care and feel like you're actually part of
              the conversation.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="closing">
            <p class="section-number">THE POINT</p>
            <h2>Your appointment. Your body. Your questions.</h2>

            <p>You don't need to become an expert in medicine.</p>

            <p>
              You don't need to walk into every appointment carrying twenty
              research papers.
            </p>

            <p>
              And you definitely don't need to prove that you know more than
              the person treating you.
            </p>

            <p>
              But understanding your health gives you the ability to ask better
              questions, recognise uncertainty and participate in decisions
              about your own care.
            </p>

            <p class="closing-line">That's the whole point of Endocation.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="disclaimer">
            <p class="eyebrow">A LITTLE ENDOCATION NOTE 💜</p>
            <p>
              This page is based on my own experiences and is intended to
              provide general educational information, not individual medical
              advice. Healthcare systems and patient rights differ depending
              on where you live, so processes such as referrals and second
              opinions may vary.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="references">
            <p class="section-number">SOURCE</p>
            <h2>Reference</h2>

            <div class="reference">
              <p>
                <strong>Becker, C.M. et al. (2022).</strong>
                <em>ESHRE guideline: endometriosis.</em>
                Human Reproduction Open, 2022(2), hoac009.
              </p>

              <a
                href="https://www.eshre.eu/Guidelines-and-Legal/Guidelines/Endometriosis-guideline"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
              >
                Read the ESHRE guideline →
              </a>
            </div>
          </div>

        </div>
      </section>
